WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:05.190
It's no surprise when filmgoers complain about
the complete lack of creativity coming from

00:00:05.190 --> 00:00:06.180
the movie industry.

00:00:06.380 --> 00:00:11.640
Still, if there's one thing even worse than
a lack of creativity, it's too much creativity,

00:00:11.960 --> 00:00:15.900
and Hollywood's history of insane abandoned
sequel ideas proves it.

00:00:16.360 --> 00:00:20.560
Since we're talking about planned sequels,
beware of spoilers for films you haven't seen

00:00:20.560 --> 00:00:21.060
yet.

00:00:22.040 --> 00:00:26.660
You'd think a franchise with 200 million years
of backstory would have no problem coming

00:00:26.660 --> 00:00:27.660
up with plots.

00:00:27.960 --> 00:00:33.760
Nevertheless, the Jurassic Park franchise
has been hyper-focused on bioengineered super-dinos.

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:39.100
The big bad in Jurassic World was the Indominus
Rex, basically a T-Rex with various other

00:00:39.110 --> 00:00:44.910
dinosaur DNA, while Jurassic World: Fallen
Kingdom featured the Indo-Raptor, a raptor

00:00:44.910 --> 00:00:46.980
crossed with Indominus Rex DNA.

00:00:47.380 --> 00:00:49.980
Believe it or not, it could have been a lot
weirder.

00:00:50.380 --> 00:00:55.820
2001's Jurassic Park III featured a low point
in the whole franchise with a dream sequence

00:00:55.820 --> 00:00:57.640
that included a talking raptor:

00:01:05.460 --> 00:01:06.100
"Alan"

00:01:08.360 --> 00:01:13.280
Despite that, the producers wanted to double
down with Human-Dino hybrids for a sequel.

00:01:13.700 --> 00:01:17.540
Yes, Jurassic Park turns into the Island of
Dr. Moreau.

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:23.480
Back In 2005, the proposed story for the fourth
film was a secret genetics lab that crossbred

00:01:23.480 --> 00:01:29.770
humans with dinosaurs, creating hybrids capable
of solving complex problems and firing automatic

00:01:29.770 --> 00:01:33.420
weapons… and probably inspiring a lot of
toys too.

00:01:33.640 --> 00:01:38.390
The idea got far enough along that a script
was written and concept art was developed.

00:01:38.390 --> 00:01:43.060
Thankfully, the human-dino hybrids idea went
extinct, but don't get too cozy.

00:01:43.240 --> 00:01:45.500
After all, to quote Dr. Ian Malcolm:

00:01:45.760 --> 00:01:48.340
"Life, uh, finds a way."

00:01:49.380 --> 00:01:53.940
Longtime Ghostbusters buffs might already
know that a potential third entry in the franchise

00:01:53.940 --> 00:01:58.560
would have found the blue-collar gang taking
a trip down to the underworld to visit Satan's

00:01:58.560 --> 00:01:59.060
homestead.

00:01:59.560 --> 00:02:04.390
The name of this film was going to be Ghostbusters
3: Hellbent, though its version of Hell would

00:02:04.390 --> 00:02:07.880
not feature your standard lake of fire and
molten brimstone.

00:02:08.640 --> 00:02:13.760
The idea was that Hell was actually an alternate
version of Manhattan — called ManHELLton

00:02:14.260 --> 00:02:18.580
— where the crew would even meet up with
the devil himself, a Donald Trump-like character

00:02:18.580 --> 00:02:20.000
named Mr. Siffler.

00:02:20.520 --> 00:02:24.480
While the "Ghostbusters in Hell" pitch is
well-known, what's less well-known is that

00:02:24.500 --> 00:02:27.840
the idea actually goes back much further,
to the first film.

00:02:28.460 --> 00:02:32.980
In early drafts of the story, the Ghostbusters
made it to the gates of Hell, which were even

00:02:32.980 --> 00:02:36.580
designed by concept artist and comic book
legend Bernie Wrightson.

00:02:37.140 --> 00:02:40.980
He elaborated on the experience in a 2016
Kickstarter video:

00:02:41.440 --> 00:02:48.879
"I did a lot of drawings of those, which I
don't know if they were filmed, but they never

00:02:48.880 --> 00:02:50.120
made it to the final movie."

00:02:51.020 --> 00:02:55.500
The original pitch for a third Indy film,
before Indiana Jones and The Last Crusade

00:02:55.500 --> 00:03:00.560
was finalized, was as crazy as its title — Indiana
Jones and the Monkey King.

00:03:01.040 --> 00:03:06.239
The story was conceived by three of the greatest
blockbuster minds ever: Steven Spielberg,

00:03:06.240 --> 00:03:09.460
George Lucas, and Home Alone director Chris
Columbus.

00:03:09.740 --> 00:03:14.580
Columbus was hired to pen the script, which
begins with Indy fighting a ghost in Scotland.

00:03:14.880 --> 00:03:19.459
Indy and friends are seeking the mythical
city of the Monkey King, whose enchanted orchard

00:03:19.460 --> 00:03:21.980
contains fruit that will grant eternal life.

00:03:22.400 --> 00:03:26.120
In typical Indy movie fashion, he must fight
Nazis along the way.

00:03:26.500 --> 00:03:32.739
In not-so-typical fashion, the Nazis have
machine gun arms and Indy's saved by tank-driving

00:03:32.740 --> 00:03:33.300
gorillas.

00:03:33.720 --> 00:03:35.900
Indy even rides a rhinoceros.

00:03:36.120 --> 00:03:37.420
It's pretty intense.

00:03:37.940 --> 00:03:43.280
Despite everything he's survived before, in
this film, Indiana Jones actually dies…

00:03:43.420 --> 00:03:44.000
temporarily.

00:03:44.580 --> 00:03:48.700
Because he's pure of heart, Indy's brought
back to life by the magical fruit of the Monkey

00:03:48.700 --> 00:03:49.320
King.

00:03:49.640 --> 00:03:54.040
Considering how insane the entire project
sounds, we might have to consider ourselves

00:03:54.060 --> 00:03:57.760
lucky that we got Indiana Jones and the Crystal
Skull instead.

00:04:06.420 --> 00:04:07.860
Well, maybe not.

00:04:08.940 --> 00:04:14.220
Will Smith's star power carried I Am Legend
to become the biggest hit of the 2007 holiday

00:04:14.220 --> 00:04:14.720
season.

00:04:15.220 --> 00:04:20.300
Nothing says holly jolly family fun like the
last man on earth battling an undead army.

00:04:20.820 --> 00:04:25.400
Given that the film made bank, Warner Bros.
naturally wanted a sequel with Smith starring.

00:04:25.780 --> 00:04:30.000
There was one problem: Will Smith's character
dies at the end of the film.

00:04:30.320 --> 00:04:35.300
Not to be deterred by a little thing like
the main character being dead, WB proceeded

00:04:35.300 --> 00:04:35.800
onward.

00:04:36.260 --> 00:04:38.120
The first solution was to make a prequel.

00:04:38.560 --> 00:04:43.590
Because I Am Legend was about the human population
being wiped out due to a zombie infection,

00:04:43.590 --> 00:04:48.020
the prequel would find Smith's lone survivor
Dr. Neville just before the world ended.

00:04:48.710 --> 00:04:52.560
Another option was floated for a sequel that
would have found Smith's character returning

00:04:52.560 --> 00:04:53.440
as a clone.

00:04:53.789 --> 00:04:58.460
Fortunately, I Am Legend 2 never got past
the planning stage, so the original remains

00:04:58.460 --> 00:04:59.060
a legend.

00:05:00.120 --> 00:05:06.340
Despite the huge success of 1977's Close Encounters
of the Third Kind, director Steven Spielberg

00:05:06.340 --> 00:05:08.060
refused to make a sequel.

00:05:08.440 --> 00:05:13.840
He offered up an alternative, though: a movie
that mixed the horror of Jaws with the extraterrestrials

00:05:13.840 --> 00:05:15.120
of Close Encounters.

00:05:15.480 --> 00:05:20.889
The film was ultimately abandoned, though
elements of it remained in E.T. the Extraterrestrial,

00:05:20.889 --> 00:05:23.480
which presents a much kinder, gentler alien.

00:05:23.700 --> 00:05:27.740
The film was a similarly huge success, and
Universal wanted a sequel.

00:05:28.060 --> 00:05:30.800
Surprisingly, Spielberg was on board for this
one.

00:05:31.160 --> 00:05:35.699
He and E.T. screenwriter Melissa Mathison
penned a treatment that had that lovable little

00:05:35.699 --> 00:05:41.810
guy E.T. being part of a warring alien race
whose evil albino members have imprisoned

00:05:41.810 --> 00:05:44.920
other alien races from across the galaxy for
food.

00:05:45.120 --> 00:05:50.340
Yes, that's exactly what Spielberg planned
for E.T. II: Nocturnal Fears.

00:05:50.720 --> 00:05:56.100
Now, E.T. isn't all sunshine, unicorns and
rainbows — it's actually a compelling examination

00:05:56.100 --> 00:06:01.040
of the effect of divorce on a family — but
still, carnivorous mutant E.T.'s?

00:06:01.240 --> 00:06:04.180
That sounds like a completely different franchise.

00:06:04.580 --> 00:06:06.980
"We better get back cause it'll get dark soon.

00:06:06.980 --> 00:06:08.650
They mostly come at night.

00:06:09.260 --> 00:06:10.080
Mostly."

00:06:11.100 --> 00:06:17.270
David Fincher's dark, serial killer film Se7en
wowed audiences and critics alike; unsurprisingly,

00:06:17.270 --> 00:06:19.020
the studio wanted a sequel.

00:06:19.340 --> 00:06:23.940
Also unsurprisingly: the sequel would have
been titled Ei8ht, with the number 8 replacing

00:06:23.940 --> 00:06:24.740
the "g."

00:06:25.100 --> 00:06:29.979
Unlike I Am Legend, which wanted to undo the
ending of the previous film, Ei8ht was content

00:06:29.980 --> 00:06:32.480
with leaving Brad Pitt's character in prison.

00:06:32.800 --> 00:06:36.800
Don't assume this means the sequel wasn't
going to be ridiculous — far from it.

00:06:37.220 --> 00:06:42.699
In Ei8ht, Morgan Freeman's world-weary detective
continues to hunt serial killers, but discovers

00:06:42.700 --> 00:06:45.240
he has the ability to predict the future.

00:06:45.540 --> 00:06:49.160
This concept got far enough along that a script
was actually written.

00:06:49.740 --> 00:06:54.340
You don't need to be see the future to know
this would have been a very bad idea.

00:06:54.760 --> 00:07:00.400
A sequel to Se7en isn't bad in theory, but
giving Freeman supernatural abilities is ludicrous.

00:07:00.740 --> 00:07:04.080
While the sequel never happened, the story
actually did.

00:07:04.220 --> 00:07:09.920
Much of the screenplay was repurposed for
2015's Solace, a movie about a psychic solving

00:07:09.920 --> 00:07:13.040
crimes starring Colin Farrell and Anthony
Hopkins.

00:07:13.240 --> 00:07:18.100
Despite the talent attached, Solace bombed,
so we don't have to worry about anyone trying

00:07:18.110 --> 00:07:19.110
to make Nine.

00:07:20.280 --> 00:07:24.600
To be fair, describing Forrest Gump's plot
makes it sound kinda nuts.

00:07:24.920 --> 00:07:31.490
Tom Hanks plays a man with a 75 IQ who experiences
almost every momentous event in American history

00:07:31.490 --> 00:07:38.000
between 1950 and 1981, from teaching Elvis
to dance to calling the police during Watergate.

00:07:38.440 --> 00:07:43.520
Still, it worked enough that it won Best Picture
and got Tom Hanks his second Oscar for Best

00:07:43.520 --> 00:07:44.020
Actor.

00:07:44.620 --> 00:07:48.840
Even with that primer, Forrest Gump II sounds
genuinely insane.

00:07:49.080 --> 00:07:50.160
Where to begin?

00:07:50.440 --> 00:07:54.219
Well, Forrest goes broke and plays for the
New Orleans Saints.

00:07:54.440 --> 00:07:59.660
He invents the formula for New Coke and also
creates a power plant fueled by pig poo.

00:07:59.780 --> 00:08:05.320
He crashes the Exxon Valdez, rides in the
back of OJ's Bronco, and works for NASA.

00:08:05.540 --> 00:08:11.040
There, he befriends an orangutan named Sue,
who joins him in Operation Desert Storm.

00:08:11.400 --> 00:08:16.080
While the first film managed to seamlessly
weave this one man throughout American history,

00:08:16.080 --> 00:08:21.400
the proposed sequel did so with all of the
subtlety of Lt. Dan hosting a children's show.

00:08:21.620 --> 00:08:23.800
The whole thing just sounds awkward.

00:08:24.360 --> 00:08:26.420
"That's all I have to say about that."

00:08:27.460 --> 00:08:32.340
Since coming home from Vietnam, John Rambo
has battled Vietnamese forces, Soviet soldiers,

00:08:32.340 --> 00:08:34.680
the Burmese army, and even some Americans.

00:08:34.880 --> 00:08:39.240
With that lineup of victories, what enemy
is left for the one-man army to take down?

00:08:39.500 --> 00:08:45.830
Well, Rambo V was originally set to pit Sylvester
Stallone's super-soldier against a half-man,

00:08:45.830 --> 00:08:47.820
half-beast, pure killing machine.

00:08:48.220 --> 00:08:53.500
Rambo V: The Savage Hunt would've seen Rambo
sent to the Pacific Northwest to clean up

00:08:53.500 --> 00:08:58.800
the mess after a top-secret government experiment
creates an out-of-control monster.

00:08:59.060 --> 00:09:02.400
Stallone clarified the movie's planned plot
in 2009:

00:09:03.120 --> 00:09:07.640
"It's not a Universal Soldier… it's not
me fighting a super soldier… it's actually

00:09:07.640 --> 00:09:08.860
a feral beast…

00:09:09.380 --> 00:09:13.820
It's this amalgamation of fury and intelligence
and pure, unadulterated rage.

00:09:14.240 --> 00:09:18.640
[Rambo's] going against a feral beast that
has absolute cunning and intelligence and

00:09:18.640 --> 00:09:21.440
a will to survive that is only matched by
Rambo's."

00:09:21.660 --> 00:09:25.800
This might be the one sequel on this list
that we almost wish did happen.

00:09:26.000 --> 00:09:31.180
Alas, Rambo V: The Savage Hunt was abandoned
in favor of the upcoming Rambo V, which will

00:09:31.180 --> 00:09:33.800
see Stallone fighting the Mexican cartel instead.

00:09:34.370 --> 00:09:38.860
Maybe, just maybe, it'll do well enough to
make Rambo vs. Monster happen.

00:09:38.870 --> 00:09:39.870
Fingers crossed!

00:09:40.870 --> 00:09:46.280
A long time ago, in a galaxy far, far away,
George Lucas made plans for a Star Wars sequel

00:09:46.280 --> 00:09:48.580
that wasn't part of his original vision.

00:09:49.000 --> 00:09:53.200
Before the movie that would eventually be
called A New Hope was released, Lucas gave

00:09:53.200 --> 00:09:57.360
partial creative control of his space opera
to writer Alan Dean Foster.

00:09:58.000 --> 00:10:03.910
Foster was hired in 1976 to pen the novelization
of Star Wars, as well as a second book which

00:10:03.910 --> 00:10:06.180
Lucas could use for a potential follow-up.

00:10:06.500 --> 00:10:11.060
That book was the first official Star Wars
sequel, Star Wars: Splinter of the Mind's

00:10:11.060 --> 00:10:11.580
Eye.

00:10:12.060 --> 00:10:16.220
Lucas' only restriction was that the story
could be filmed on a low budget so he could

00:10:16.220 --> 00:10:17.900
reuse props and sets.

00:10:18.380 --> 00:10:24.180
Splinter finds Luke, Leia, C-3PO, and R2-D2
stranded on a dark, foggy mining world which

00:10:24.180 --> 00:10:26.810
contains a powerful crystal sought by the
Empire.

00:10:26.810 --> 00:10:31.500
Vader shows up only briefly for the grand
finale fight, while Han and Chewie don't show

00:10:31.500 --> 00:10:36.020
up at all, as Harrison Ford was only contracted
for one Star Wars.

00:10:36.460 --> 00:10:41.420
Splinter is a cool idea, but was basically
Lucas' backup plan in case Star Wars only

00:10:41.420 --> 00:10:42.520
did so-so.

00:10:43.150 --> 00:10:46.880
Of course, it became the highest-grossing
film of all time at that point, launching

00:10:46.890 --> 00:10:51.960
arguably the most popular franchise in history
and allowing Lucas to film his saga as he

00:10:51.960 --> 00:10:53.720
had planned it from the start.

00:10:54.860 --> 00:10:58.500
What is it with sequels to movies where the
main character dies?

00:10:58.820 --> 00:11:03.940
While Gladiator may not have aged well, Ridley
Scott's 2000 swords-and-sandals epic took

00:11:03.940 --> 00:11:06.140
home Best Picture and box office gold.

00:11:06.480 --> 00:11:11.310
Russell Crowe also won Best Actor for his
performance as Maximus Decimus Meridius, his

00:11:11.310 --> 00:11:14.840
crowning moment in a career that has been
spotty at times.

00:11:15.150 --> 00:11:20.040
One of those spotty times was in the mid-to-late
2000s, after the biggest bomb of his and Ridley

00:11:20.040 --> 00:11:24.100
Scott's respective careers, 2006's A Good
Year.

00:11:24.540 --> 00:11:29.360
Perhaps hoping to revive past glory, Crowe
wanted to be in a Gladiator sequel, despite

00:11:29.360 --> 00:11:31.480
Maximus dying at the end of the film.

00:11:31.720 --> 00:11:32.660
The solution?

00:11:32.880 --> 00:11:35.060
Just throw some Roman mythology on it.

00:11:35.360 --> 00:11:39.580
Scott and Crowe hired Australian musician
and screenwriter Nick Cave to come up with

00:11:39.580 --> 00:11:42.410
a creative solution to bring back Maximus.

00:11:42.820 --> 00:11:47.780
Cave's Gladiator 2 had Maximus melding with
Roman gods to achieve immortality.

00:11:48.360 --> 00:11:52.700
He is then reincarnated on Earth, where he
defends the early Christians and goes on to

00:11:52.700 --> 00:11:58.460
fight in various conflicts, including World
War II and the Vietnam War, eventually rising

00:11:58.460 --> 00:12:00.740
through the ranks of the modern-day Pentagon.

00:12:01.200 --> 00:12:04.410
The film might not have been good, but it
definitely sounds entertaining.

00:12:05.540 --> 00:12:06.720
"Are you not entertained?

00:12:07.560 --> 00:12:09.320
Are you not entertained?

00:12:11.920 --> 00:12:13.320
Is this not why you are here?"

